# What It Costs to Trust a Source: A Reproducible Method for Institutional Assessment of Author Disambiguation

Simon J. Porter 1, *∗*

1*Digital Science, 6 Briset Street, London EC1M 5NR, United Kingdom* (Dated: 29 October 2026)

Records of research outputs are curated with care by universities, and the effort is shaped by the external sources from which candidate records are drawn. The curation required before the author identities in such sources can be trusted is examined, and a reproducible method for estimating its cost is set out. Four tests are described; two can be run on public data alone, and two are conducted against an institution’s own curated record, taken from Symplectic Elements Discovery portals. The method is demonstrated on OpenAlex and Dimensions across 30 institutions. Of the publications attributed to matched researchers, 50.4% were confirmed by the institutional record for OpenAlex and 69.6% for Dimensions, and fewer trusted researcher profiles were retained from OpenAlex at a matched confidence threshold in each of the 30 institutions. Carried through to effort, integrating OpenAlex requires 1.5 times the person-days of review and entry of Dimensions even when each feed is filtered at the threshold that minimises the amount of review required.

**Keywords:** author disambiguation; research information management; CRIS; ORCID; OpenAlex; Dimensions

**I. INTRODUCTION**

Since the early 2000s, bibliographic sources on the web have helped universities to assemble records of the research of their staff. In most research intensive institutions Current Research Information Systems (CRIS) now support compliance and assessment submissions, open-access monitoring, annual reporting and the public profiles through which an institution presents its people to collaborators, funders and the press \[7\]. The outcomes based on these uses are consequential for both individuals and institutions and hence records are curated in collaboration and with care by research offices, librarians and researchers. This activity is both ongoing and significant and hence the ongoing cost of curation is a recurring concern in the literature on research information management \[2\].

The scholarly records upon which this curation is based have been built in layers: Crossref has registered DOIs and publication metadata that has been deposited by source publishers since 2000 \[21\] and has made efforts to include historic content; and DataCite, founded in 2009 as a separate DOI registration agency, brought datasets, and later other research outputs, into the DOI system \[5\]. ORCID launched in 2012 to give research participants a public record of their outputs that they maintain themselves

*∗* [s.porter@digital-science.com](mailto:s.porter@digital-science.com);

https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6151-8423

\[20\]. In each of these registries an assertion is made by a party with direct knowledge of a fact concerning a scholarly record, and each claim carries clear provenance, in the sense of FAIR principle R1.2 \[69\]. To be explicit, we interpret this to mean not only that every metadata item should conform to the FAIR principles, but that within reason, the provenance of the assertion should be implicitly clear. That is to say that, for example, metadata vested on Crossref can be assumed to be findable by virtue of Crossref’s data services; accessible through Crossref’s use of standardised, open data formats; interoperable as a result of the aforementioned formats; and reusable not only due to Crossref’s licence terms but also implicitly because the record is trustworthy, being as provided to Crossref by its originator. This trust in: i) faithful reproduction of the record from a source; ii) the ability to assume such provenance for a whole record without the need to check individually; and iii) the knowledge that records have not been added or deleted and that the data provided by Crossref constitutes an unfiltered and unredacted set is critical to all the layers that then derive from it. A second layer of sources, among them Web of Science, Scopus and Dimensions \[67\] and, more recently, OpenAlex \[50\], constructs knowledge that the registries do not hold: algorithmic identifiers for researchers whose names appear only as text, affiliations resolved from address strings, and classifications by field.

In this context a *secondary source* is an index that derives assertions about authorship algorithmically from metadata that it did not itself author, whether or not those assertions are later curated by hand. By a *person-level record* we mean the attribution of research outputs to a specific individual, expressed as an author identifier together with the outputs gathered under it, as distinct from the documentlevel record of what was published, where and with what citations, on which most comparisons of sources have concentrated. There are two kinds of failure that are common but which are not equally visible. A missing research output in a person’s record is a gap that someone eventually notices, whereas a researcher profile that contains another person’s work looks to every downstream system like a richer profile, even if the researcher appears to be publishing in a different domain with different collaborators, this is not unusual - many researchers change fields during their career—it might also mark a transistion to a different field of research. These “richer” profiles have an increasingly consequential impact due to the growing class of applications, from research-intelligence platforms that match researchers to policy-engagement opportunities \[43\] to integrity screens that flag anomalous publication behaviour \[27, 47\], and all these applications broadly take author identities as given and author disambiguation if not as a solved problem then a sufficiently solved problem.

An institution that brings such a source into its CRIS therefore takes on work that is not usually included in the calculation of the cost of ownership. We call it the *integration cost*, and measure it in effort: the curation that must be layered on top of a source before its claims about the institution’s own people can be acted upon. Both the level of curation and where that curation lands is a matter of choice - informed by institutional and national cultural norms, financial considerations, trust in systems, trust in data, local ethical and regulatory practices, risk tolerance, and intended data use. Effort spent correcting a secondary source repairs one copy of the record inside another organisation’s database, whereas confirmed person–publication links maintained in the institution’s own CRIS fix the institutional record and, where connected through ORCID, supply an authority from which every downstream index can draw.

This paper sets out a reproducible method for estimating the integration cost of a scientometric source from data that an adopting institution already holds. Two of its four tests are *source-level*, using only a source’s output and the ORCID public data file to assess how likely the source is to conflate distinct researchers; two are *institutional*, reading the source against an institution’s own curated record as a proxy for a golden dataset. The method is demonstrated on OpenAlex \[50\] and Dimensions \[22, 24\], two indexes built on open persistent-identifier infrastructure and openly available metadata, across 30 institutions whose records are exposed through Symplectic Elements Discovery portals. The results are expressed in person-days, for administrators reviewing a feed and for researchers reviewing their own suggestions.

