**Figure 1:** Uses of the institutional publication record, ordered by the distance of the decision from the individual researcher. The schematic is qualitative: the positions are ordinal and the crossing point is illustrative rather than measured.

The publication data held by a university are put to uses that differ considerably in how much error they can tolerate (Figure 1). At one extreme sits the case for promotion. In this case records are read by a committee whose decision bears directly on an individual’s career; a misattributed or missing paper may materially affect the outcome and is a matter on which the individual might reasonably seek redress. The grant application is not far removed. A funder will expect an accurate account of the outcomes of earlier awards and of the work of each investigator named in the proposal, and an error reflects not only on the individuals concerned but on the standing of the institution with that funder.

Reporting to government occupies a middle position. Accuracy is still required, but most evaluative systems treat the data in aggregate, where an individual misattribution is diluted within the totals of a department or an institution; an exercise such as the UK’s Research Excellence Framework, in which named outputs are submitted against named staff, sits rather closer to the stricter end than its aggregate presentation might suggest. At national level the data are used to discern trends: whether a field is growing, whether investment should follow, and how a research system aligns with a government’s view of critical technologies. These are among the most consequential allocations made from such data, and yet the least sensitive to the accuracy of any individual record.

It follows that the bodies with the greatest influence over the direction of research funding are, in some sense, the least exposed to the consequences of inaccurate attribution. A national decision stands one step removed from the institutions that carry its consequences and two steps removed from the individuals whose records underlie it, while the cost of curation, and the risk that attends its absence, fall on research offices and researchers.

Aggregation is forgiving of random error, though rather less so of systematic error, and profile conflation is of the latter kind. It concentrates on common names, which may be expected to cluster by naming convention and hence by country and region, and possibly by field, which are the units that national analyses compare, so whether a source’s errors lean in one direction also matters beyond the research office. For the institution itself, the confidence required of a source becomes a property of the use to which the data are put, and a single institution will in practice need several levels of confidence from the same feed. This paper is organised as follows: We provide a brief literature review (Section II) before describing the data and test methodology in Sec. III. Sec. IV contains our results, after which we discuss their implications in Sec. V) and note the limitations of our study in Sec. VI.

**II. RELATED WORK**

**The cost of research information.** The uses of a CRIS have expanded steadily, from compliance and assessment through open-access monitoring and research-data registration to public researcher profiles \[7, 53\]. Increasingly the CRIS also underpins what might be called *university-in-the-loop* reporting: measurement grounded in records that the institution itself verifies and warrants, in contrast to rankings and evaluations computed entirely from secondary sources outside its control. The Barcelona Declaration on Open Research Information reinforces this direction, committing its signatories to make openness the default for the research information they use, including to assess researchers, and to require that the data in research information systems can be exported and made open, so that the information used in evaluation is accessible and auditable by those being assessed \[4\], commitments that apply directly to institutional records such as those studied here. A registry that drives public profiles and institutionally warranted reporting must be correct at the level of the individual researcher, and the literature is consistent that such correctness is bought with labour: profiling and cleansing routines \[3\], quality problems reported across national contexts \[2\], a standard of fitness for purpose that rises as uses accumulate \[19\], and work on integrating repositories and workflows \[52\]. What has been missing is a measurement connecting this literature to the choice of external source: how far the curation burden of a CRIS is determined in advance by the disambiguation quality of the source that feeds it, and whether that burden differs between sources consistently enough to be weighed in the choice.

**The effort of record entry and verification.** No published study, to our knowledge, measures how long it takes to accept or reject a publication that a CRIS suggests, so the effort of integration has to be assembled from adjacent tasks. For entering a record by hand, those who run a CRIS work with figures of a few minutes: the Portuguese national CV platform assumes three minutes per manually entered record in estimating the effort saved by its suggestion service \[16\], a case study of open-access workflows in Symplectic Elements estimates three minutes to create a record with its full text \[58\], and repository guidance allows five to ten minutes for a complete deposit \[66\].

Confirming an existing record has also been observed to be quicker than creating one. In a timedriven study of cataloguing, adapting an existing bibliographic description took 74 seconds against 306 seconds to create one, about three-quarters less, although across the whole workflow, physical processing included, the saving was a third \[55\]. Screening for systematic reviews supplies the nearest logged measurements of a quick bibliographic judgement: a mean of 35 seconds per abstract over nearly 400,000 decisions on one platform \[26\], and medians of 15 to 20 seconds in a small experiment reported by a vendor \[35\]. These are judgements of relevance against inclusion criteria rather than of authorship, and the full-text assessment that a doubtful case can require has been put, from one team’s experience, at about fifteen minutes \[65\].

Administrative records may be maintained by researchers as well as administrators. A researcher who reviews suggestions personally also bears the overhead of logging into each session. Applying published second-factor times of roughly 10 to 21 seconds after the password to logs of more than 35 million two-factor logins at two universities, Reynolds et al. \[51\] find more than one login attempt in twenty abandoned or failed at the second step, and locate the burden less in the total time than in the position of the step on the critical path to the task the user came to do. Administration can also be considered to be an interruption to researchers’ other responsibilities. Without supplying a cost per session, research on interruption finds that interrupted work is often not resumed for tens of minutes, during which other work intervenes \[28, 32\], and people compensate for interruption by working faster while reporting greater stress \[33\]. Estimating the effort that research-information workflows consume, and the share of it that persistent identifiers can remove, has also been developed at national scale \[6\]. **Comparing scientometric sources.** OpenAlex \[50\] inherited both the corpus and the ambition of
